{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310632,"nonce":"BFFdi55fpZkMOLLXVdzesImFEhA=","title":"2026-09-19 Int64 batch (TestInt64 COMPILE_FAIL -\u003e testGen only) — hex literals wrap to Int, i64 suffix desugars to Int64.make, value ...","message":"Batch after a37fcfc1, git-bug 33d9aca 9dc5814 8f0e788 2b2dab7 8df84c7 (fixed) + c40a78a (Null\u003cInt64\u003e) 7a6f8f5 (Vector new) open.\n\n**What was wrong (all pre-existing, none in any failure list until TestInt64 compiled):**\n- Literals: both parsers kept `0xFFFFFFFF` as i64 4294967295. Haxe = Int32.of_string: hex/bin/octal within 32 bits wrap to Int; decimal past Int (or anything past 32 bits) is Float; `-2147483648` is Int; `Xi64` suffix (Haxe 4.3) → now `haxe.Int64.make(high, low)` in the parser (`int_literal_kind`, shared by RD and legacy).\n- `a.toInt()` on an Int64 VALUE reached a STATIC mapping row through three probes that drop the receiver and zero-fill → 0 for every input. Rule: a static row reached through a value (`!is_class_symbol_expr(receiver)`, arity fits) takes the value as arg 0.\n- `Int64_toInt` wrapper truncated; Haxe throws \"Overflow\". Removed the row → exposed the LAST-RESORT all-classes scan in `try_stdlib_runtime_dispatch` (toInt/0 args → `Usize_toInt`). Gated: an owner-carrying symbol never reaches it.\n- `tryOverflow.bind(a)` in an instance method: body was `FunctionCall(Variable(method))` with no receiver → env slot 0 read as `this`, arg zero-filled. Now `MethodCall(this, ...)`; Void bind body = call + bare return.\n- `var a:Int64; a = make(..); a = 1` with `a` captured: 32-bit store into the i64 cell — the local's declared type was the CLASS under the abstract, so no @:from fired. `maybe_abstract_from_convert` widens any integer register bound for an Int64 spelling (by REGISTER type, not declared source type).\n- `eq(a.high, 0)`: `high` is typed `Int32` (abstract, module lowers late) → typer left it Dynamic → raw I32 into a Dynamic slot (0 printed null, 0x80000000 deref'd). Fixed at the CALL BOUNDARY only (`box_scalar_register` in helpers/signatures.rs).\n- `Int64.ofInt(d)` with `d` a `Null\u003cInt\u003e` local passed the box pointer → parseString garbage. `maybe_box_for_extern_call` unboxes ONLY registers in `boxed_value_regs`.\n- Sibling defaults `b:Int = a` / `this.f`: lowered at the call site with the callee's param symbols unbound → 0. Now `function_param_symbols` binds earlier args (+ SymbolId(0) = this) while the default lowers.\n\n**Why the first gate run LOST 10 rows (547→537):** two rules were too broad. (1) \"Dynamic-typed value whose register is a scalar → box\" inside `maybe_box_value_inner` broke `var f2:Dynamic = getValue(); f2 % 100` (raw-f64-in-Dynamic-local convention, Issue3447) and Std.string of an abstract-with-toString (Issue2401, I had also made the TYPER box abstracts-over-Int). (2) \"Ptr register into a scalar extern param → unbox\" broke every ERASED GENERIC value (raw bits in a Ptr-typed register; Issue5455 `protect\u003cT\u003e` SIGSEGV at fault 0x1) and every I64 slot (erased pointers). Narrowed to boundary-only / boxed_value_regs-only; the crashes in `StringBuf.grow` etc. all went away.\n\n**How to apply:** a representation rule (\"box this\", \"unbox that\") must be keyed on something the lowering KNOWS (`boxed_value_regs`, a call-boundary formal), never on the HIR type or the register type alone — both lie for erased generics and for Dynamic locals. Re-run the LOST rows individually before the full gate; `Issue4986` PASS at HEAD was DCE luck over a load from address 1 (`new Vector(1)` = 1).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_int64_batch_literals_receivers_defaults` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}